# a-wall-cut-from-sand — BACKLOG C6, and it is the BOTTOM of a triple.
#
# `a-base-the-player-builds.keys` with one token changed on five lines:
# the band is painted `sand` instead of `grass`, so the wall the crew
# raise is cut from sand and is worth half of one cut from grass.
#
#     palette 4  →  the band is SAND    falls at 153
#     palette 5  →  the band is GRASS   falls at 174   (`@M050`)
#     palette 7  →  the band is ROCK    falls at 220   (`@M061`)
#
# ⚠ **A wall on sand buys +23 ticks where the same wall on grass buys
# +44** — half, which is `FOOTING_BRITTLE` arriving in the clock.
#
# ── ⚠⚠ Why the whole band has to be sand ────────────────────────
#
# `footing_of` takes the STURDIEST ground the wall touches — *the crew
# cut the best stone they can reach* — so one hex of sand beside a wall
# changes nothing at all, and a probe measured exactly that: the same
# scenario with a single sand column next to the wall line falls at
# **174**, to the tick.
#
# ⚠⚠ That is the cost of a maximum, and it is deliberate: the mean and
# the minimum both make a standing wall get WEAKER when the wall beside
# it breaks, because a break paints ordinary ground where a wall used to
# be.  **Brittleness is therefore a property of a REGION and never of a
# hex**, which is what *sand rock is brittle* has to mean to be worth
# saying.

echo a wall cut from sand: the same wall, on ground worth half as much

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 4
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80
# The band is sand from end to end — the ONE token, and the only thing
# that differs from `a-base-the-player-builds.keys`.
kind 8 0 sand

# The crew, sitting on the line the player is about to draw.
crew 5 -1
crew 5 1

# ── The build (or not) ──────────────────────────────────────────
park 5 -2
paint on
drive 5 2
tick 6
count orders 5 5
paint off

# Park clear of the line so the trail is not rubbed out, and let the
# crew work.  60 helper-seconds over two helpers is 30 s = 45 ticks.
drive 0 0
tick 50
count orders 0 0
count painted 80 80
# ⚠⚠ The whole band, hex by hex — because a wall with ONE gap in it
# buys nothing at all (`plans/12` § B7 measured a gate at +1 tick), so
# *five* is the number that matters rather than *some*.
kind 5 -2 wall
kind 5 -1 wall
kind 5 0 wall
kind 5 1 wall
kind 5 2 wall

# ⚠⚠ **The mechanic, read exactly, before a single robot arrives.**
#
# ⚠ A COLUMN is not a straight run on this lattice: constant `q` with
# `r` varying ZIGZAGS, because row parity flips which delta each
# direction index carries, so every middle hex is a turning pair and
# takes `BRACE_FULL` (`damage.loft` § "Straight" is a DIRECTION-INDEX
# opposition).  A wall a player drags down the screen is a
# crinkle-crankle wall and is stronger for it.
#
# So the middle of this column is the full `wall_hp` times its footing:
# **100 on grass, 50 on sand, 200 on rock.**  This one is 50.
hp 5 0 50 50


# ── The waves — identical to `an-undefended-base` ───────────────
wave 5
count alive 5 5

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

# ── The clock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ **130 against 174 — the wall the player DREW is worth +44 ticks,
# a third of the base's life** (`@M050`).  Same ground, same core, same
# spawn, same wave list, same crew, same drive, same ticks spent — one
# token apart.
fall 900
ticks 149 157

count alive 13 13
snap c6_a_wall_cut_from_sand
